const periodCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Blocks with intent', note: 'Periodisation: divide the year into macro (season), meso (3–6 week blocks) and micro (week) cycles, each with a target — hypertrophy, strength, power, peak. The alternative (training everything equally, always) leaves you average at everything.' },
    { math: '2 · The classic models', note: 'Linear (heavy to peaking — simple, works for beginners), undulating (daily variation — flexible for team sports), block (concentrated sequenced loads — elite athletes). The science verdict: all work if progressive overload and recovery are programmed; specificity of model matters less than adherence (the honest finding).' },
    { math: '3 · Tapering: arriving fresh', note: 'Reduce volume 40–60% over 1–3 weeks, keep intensity — fitness decays slowly, fatigue decays fast (the fitness-fatigue model from the training lesson); the taper reveals the shape the training built. The best-timed taper is worth more than the last hard week.' },
    { math: '4 · Monitoring: the programme\'s dashboard', note: 'Load (session-RPE × minutes — Foster\'s method), wellness questionnaires, HRV, jump testing (a 5% drop flags fatigue) — the data-science lesson\'s dashboard thinking, physiological edition: adjust the plan when the athlete\'s data says so.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/sports-science/advanced/periodisation-and-programming">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Periodisation is the training lesson\'s supercompensation, <strong>orchestrated across a
    season</strong>: sequencing stress, recovery and peaking so the athlete arrives at the
    start line at maximum preparedness — which is a scheduling problem as much as a
    physiological one.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the blocks, the models, the taper, monitoring" widget="equationStep" config={periodCfg} />

  <h2>The practitioner\'s realities</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Acute:chronic workload ratio:</strong> the injury-risk debate (Gabbett\'s sweet spot ~0.8–1.3) — spikes after lazy pre-seasons injure; the ratio is a useful warning light even as its causal interpretation remains argued (the statistics lesson\'s ecological-fallacy caution applies).</li>
    <li><strong>Team sports complicate everything:</strong> fixtures don\'t follow the plan (midweek Champions League ruins your mesocycle) — in-season maintenance blocks and small-sided games keep concurrent goals (fitness + tactics + freshness) balanced.</li>
    <li><strong>Individual response varies:</strong> same programme, 2× spread in hypertrophy response (the genetics lesson\'s variance, in the gym) — coaching is adjusting the template to the responder, which is why monitoring beats any off-the-shelf plan.</li>
    <li><strong>Detraining is fast and specific:</strong> 2 weeks off costs aerobic capacity measurably; strength holds longer — return-to-training after injury must rebuild load gradually (the tissue-remodelling lesson of the training lesson: tendons adapt slower than muscle).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Matveyev (the classical model); Gabbett on workload; Issurin on block periodisation; the training-load lesson.</p>
</TopicPage>
